WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.600
With NAT, three kinds of address translations&nbsp;
can occur as a request from a private address&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.640
is attached to a public IP address. First, we&nbsp;
have NAT that takes place where every private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.600
IP address that makes a request gets a public&nbsp;
IP address to use. This process is a rare way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.760
to do NAT as most homes and businesses&nbsp;
have only one public IP address to use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.720
Another way to do NAT is to use a NAT pool&nbsp;
where a private IP address request uses an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.680
available public IP address from a pool of IP&nbsp;
addresses. This process can be problematic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:37.760
Say there are five public IP addresses in a pool&nbsp;
and 20 devices with private IP addresses making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.720
requests, then 15 devices will need to wait&nbsp;
for a public IP address to become available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:50.160
The most common form of NAT is Port NAT, often&nbsp;
known as Port Address Translation. In this setup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.680
each private IP address request gets assigned a&nbsp;
port from a public IP address. This combination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:02.160
of public IP address and port is used to send&nbsp;
a request for data to another network. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.320
when the data is received, the router knows&nbsp;
what port number matches up with the private IP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.840
address requesting the data, and then the data&nbsp;
is sent to the device that requested the data.

